Jacobs wrote:

> Benjamin Lewis <bclewis@cs.sfu.ca> wrote:
>> Kent Paul Dolan wrote:
>>> Unix "wizard mode" is all about being able to install and uninstall
>>> games, grant and revoke user privileges, mess with wrapper scripts
>>> and records logs and upgrades and backups.
>>
>> I tend to agree, however, that allowing regular users to run in wizard
>> mode is a reasonable thing to do, *provided* that wiz bones can't be
>> loaded by regular characters.
>
> Personally when I think multi-user I think of more than one user on one
> computer as opposed to nethack.alt.org and in that case I certainly think
> that regular users should be allowed to use wizard mode.  
>
> Actually, in that situation I'd let them load wiz bones by regular
> characters anyway, because I do NOT want to share a computer with someone
> who breaks rules for their convenience...who knows what other bad things
> they're doing to my computer for their convenience. 

Are you suggesting removing wizmode restrictions, and relying on the
honesty of other people on a multi-user system to not leave bones files?

One of the main benefits of multi-user systems such as unix is that regular
users *can't* do "bad things" to your computer.

> It's only on a server that allows nethack users to play together on their
> own single user systems that I would even think about such a restriction.

Strictly speaking, they're not playing on their own single user systems,
they're playing on the server, via telnet or ssh *from* their single user
system.  In the case of something like nethack.alt.org, it's simple enough
to disallow wizard mode regardless of account restrictions, since the users
are not running NetHack from the command line, and thus cannot type
something such as "nethack -u wizard -D", even if ordinary users on the
system could do this.  

For this reason, you can not start a game in eXplore mode on
nethack.alt.org either.  (I believe you can switch to eXplore mode while
playing, but then you don't get the wand of wishing).

